From a960529352fb4ea27902fd352fe576407ae77891 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SorinS <ssuciu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 23:08:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] -g: a debug map beside the C, <stem>.debug.json

The C's debug information gives a debugger addresses, lines and C types,
not what the C types stand for: a resizable is a bare header, a
variable-size value a bare pointer, T& and T? one pointer type, bool a
u8, a function instance area_g_2, and every local is in scope from the
function's top. Under -g the driver writes beside the C a JSON map
(codegen_debugmap.h has the format):

- each function instance: its C name, Goose qualified name, file, lines,
  generic bindings, and what each function-value parameter is bound to;
- each of its locals and parameters: C name, Goose name, Goose type and
  the lines it is in scope, from its declaration to the end of the
  innermost block around it;
- the globals: each one's member of gs_globals_t, Goose name and type,
  and whether threads reach their own copy through gs_gl;
- each struct and enum those types name: its C name where the program
  has one, its fields, or its variants with their tags and fields.

The map is collected as the C is generated and changes none of it: C
type names are recorded where NameCT makes them, never asked for.

Test: the C is byte-identical with and without the map; every codegen
test's map is valid JSON; an LLDB module reading it shows locals and
globals by Goose name, type and scope, resizables' elements, variable-
size values decoded, tag names and function-value instances.
